<h1>Getting Started</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Install mcp-librarian, run the installer, verify the setup, and perform your first skill search.</p>

    <h2 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h2>

    <p>mcp-librarian requires <strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Node.js 18 or later</strong>. No other dependencies are needed -- the project is built entirely on Node.js built-in modules. There is no <code>npm install</code> step.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Check your Node.js version</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">node --version</span>
<span class="dim">v22.4.0</span></div>

    <p>You also need <strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Git</strong> for cloning the repository, and an MCP-compatible client (Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf, or a local model runner with MCP support).</p>

    <h2 id="installation">Installation</h2>

    <p>Clone the repository and run the installer. Three commands, nothing else.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">git clone https://github.com/penumbraforge/mcp-librarian.git</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">cd mcp-librarian</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">node bin/install.js</span></div>

    <p>The installer is interactive and handles everything automatically. No <code>npm install</code> is required because mcp-librarian has zero external dependencies.</p>

    <h2 id="what-the-installer-does">What the Installer Does</h2>

    <p>The installer performs four operations in sequence:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Generates Ed25519 signing keys.</strong> A keypair is created at <code>~/.mcp-librarian/keys/</code>. The private key signs every skill on write. The public key verifies integrity on every load. See <a href="/librarian/wiki/signing/">Ed25519 Signing</a> for details.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Creates the home directory.</strong> The default location is <code>~/.mcp-librarian/</code>. This directory holds your skills, search index, keys, and exports. See <a href="/librarian/wiki/configuration/">Configuration</a> for the full directory structure.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Detects your MCP client.</strong> The installer scans for Claude Code, Cursor, and Windsurf configuration files. If it finds one (or more), it offers to configure them automatically.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Configures the MCP client.</strong> The installer writes the correct MCP server configuration to your client's config file, pointing it to the mcp-librarian entry point with the correct arguments.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">node bin/install.js</span>

<span class="dim">  mcp-librarian installer</span>

<span class="ok">  Generated Ed25519 keypair</span>
<span class="dim">  Private key: ~/.mcp-librarian/keys/signing.key</span>
<span class="dim">  Public key:  ~/.mcp-librarian/keys/signing.pub</span>

<span class="ok">  Created home directory</span>
<span class="dim">  Location: ~/.mcp-librarian/</span>

<span class="ok">  Detected MCP client: Claude Code</span>
<span class="dim">  Config: ~/.claude/mcp_servers.json</span>

<span class="ok">  Configured mcp-librarian for Claude Code</span>
<span class="dim">  Ready to use. Restart your editor to activate.</span></div>

    <h2 id="manual-configuration">Manual Configuration</h2>

    <p>If the installer does not detect your MCP client, or you prefer manual setup, add the following to your client's MCP configuration file.</p>

    <h3>Claude Code</h3>
    <p>Add to <code>~/.claude/mcp_servers.json</code>:</p>

    <div class="code-block">{
  <span class="kw">"mcp-librarian"</span>: {
    <span class="kw">"command"</span>: <span class="str">"node"</span>,
    <span class="kw">"args"</span>: [<span class="str">"/path/to/mcp-librarian/src/index.js"</span>],
    <span class="kw">"env"</span>: {}
  }
}</div>

    <h3>Cursor</h3>
    <p>Add to your Cursor MCP settings (<code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code>):</p>

    <div class="code-block">{
  <span class="kw">"mcpServers"</span>: {
    <span class="kw">"mcp-librarian"</span>: {
      <span class="kw">"command"</span>: <span class="str">"node"</span>,
      <span class="kw">"args"</span>: [<span class="str">"/path/to/mcp-librarian/src/index.js"</span>]
    }
  }
}</div>

    <h3>Windsurf</h3>
    <p>Add to your Windsurf MCP configuration:</p>

    <div class="code-block">{
  <span class="kw">"mcpServers"</span>: {
    <span class="kw">"mcp-librarian"</span>: {
      <span class="kw">"command"</span>: <span class="str">"node"</span>,
      <span class="kw">"args"</span>: [<span class="str">"/path/to/mcp-librarian/src/index.js"</span>]
    }
  }
}</div>

    <p>Replace <code>/path/to/mcp-librarian</code> with the actual path where you cloned the repository.</p>

    <h2 id="verifying-the-setup">Verifying the Setup</h2>

    <p>After installation, restart your editor or MCP client to pick up the new server configuration. Then verify that mcp-librarian is running by calling the <code>server_status</code> tool.</p>

    <p>In Claude Code, you can simply ask:</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="dim">"Check if mcp-librarian is running."</span>

<span class="cmt"># The agent will call server_status and return something like:</span>

<span class="ok">  mcp-librarian v3.1.0</span>
<span class="dim">  Skills:    0</span>
<span class="dim">  Uptime:    3s</span>
<span class="dim">  Index:     empty (no skills loaded)</span>
<span class="dim">  Home:      ~/.mcp-librarian</span>
<span class="dim">  Signing:   Ed25519 keypair loaded</span></div>

    <p>If you see a version number, skill count, and signing status, the server is running correctly. The skill count will be 0 until you install packs or create skills.</p>

    <h2 id="first-skill-search">Your First Skill Search</h2>

    <p>With a fresh installation, the library is empty. The fastest way to populate it is to install a community pack. Ask your agent:</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="dim">"Install the security-hardening pack."</span>

<span class="cmt"># The agent calls install_pack({ pack: "security-hardening" })</span>

<span class="ok">  Installed pack: security-hardening</span>
<span class="dim">  Skills added: 7</span>
<span class="dim">  Index rebuilt: 7 skills, 42 sections</span></div>

    <p>Now try a search. Ask your agent about any security topic:</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="dim">"What are the best practices for input validation?"</span>

<span class="cmt"># The agent calls find_and_load({ query: "input validation" })</span>
<span class="cmt"># BM25 search finds the matching skill and returns the relevant section.</span></div>

    <p>The agent receives the skill content and uses it to inform its response. You never need to explicitly ask the agent to check the library -- it does this automatically when it recognizes a relevant topic from the skill catalog.</p>

    <h2 id="auto-research-example">Auto-Research in Action</h2>

    <p>If you ask about a topic with no matching skill, auto-research activates automatically:</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="dim">"Help me set up GraphQL federation."</span>

<span class="cmt"># The agent calls find_and_load({ query: "GraphQL federation" })</span>
<span class="cmt"># No skill found -- auto-research triggers</span>
<span class="cmt"># Searches the web, scores sources by authority</span>
<span class="cmt"># Returns research from Apollo docs, graphql.org, and more</span>
<span class="cmt"># Agent creates a skill with create_skill</span>
<span class="cmt"># Permanently indexed -- next time, instant hit</span></div>

    <p>See <a href="/librarian/wiki/auto-research/">Auto-Research</a> for the full breakdown of how source scoring and research works.</p>

